# 0001. Agentic documentation system

- Status: accepted
- Date: {{DATE}}

## Context

AI agents work in this repository. Without written rules they re-derive conventions from the code on every session, guess at prohibitions that no code expresses, and repeat mistakes already paid for.

Two failure modes had to be avoided. A file too generic wastes the instruction budget stating what the model already knows, and measurably degrades task success. A file too large stops being followed at all: adherence degrades past the instruction budget ({{BUDGET_FIGURE}}).

## Decision

A tier-{{TIER}} system: {{STRUCTURE_SUMMARY}}.

Content rules:

- Behaviour lives in the always-loaded root file and is never externalised.
- Guardrails keep their prohibition in the root file; rationale and procedure go to the domain file, making their cost conditional.
- Nothing is written that was not verified: every documented command was executed, every cited path exists.
- Rules that a script can check have one. {{GUARDRAIL_SCRIPTS}}

## Consequences

Documentation must be updated in the same commit as the code that invalidates it, or it decays into confidently wrong instructions, which are worse than none.

The instruction budget is finite: past the threshold, a rule added displaces one already there. Adding a rule means asking what it replaces.

## Alternatives considered

**One large file.** Simpler to navigate, but every session pays for every domain. Rejected past tier 1.

**Per-domain files only, no root file.** Loses the cross-cutting rules that must apply on every turn regardless of the file being touched.

**Generated from the code on demand.** Cannot express prohibitions, which is precisely the non-inferable part.
